Subject: [PATCH] do not use the freed last buffer when a session ends

reader_func() frees each buffer once it has been written.  When the
session ends, it then takes that same buffer again (buf = sbuf), splits it
once more when a SID was asked for, and writes the splitter's whole buffer
(buffer_size, not what was filled).  With a SID, closing an http client
made the server read freed memory and end (seen on the SB-TV06-WRIP).

Only a decoder may still hold data at that point: start from an empty
buffer, and write what the splitter filled.

---
diff --git a/recpt1/recpt1.c b/recpt1/recpt1.c
index 426e08f..eca3ab6 100644
--- a/recpt1/recpt1.c
+++ b/recpt1/recpt1.c
@@ -462,7 +462,10 @@ reader_func(void *p)
         /* normal exit */
         if((f_exit && !p_queue->num_used) || file_err) {
 
-            buf = sbuf; /* default */
+            /* The last buffer was written and freed above (sbuf points into
+             * it): what is left is only what a decoder still holds. */
+            buf.data = NULL;
+            buf.size = 0;
 
             if(use_b25) {
                 code = b25_finish(dec, &sbuf, &dbuf);
@@ -485,7 +488,7 @@ reader_func(void *p)
                 }
 
                 buf.data = splitbuf.buffer;
-                buf.size = splitbuf.buffer_size;
+                buf.size = splitbuf.buffer_filled;
             }
 
             if(!fileless && !file_err) {
